[38;5;246m  ⎿  · What kind of app is this task layer for? → Server-backed multi-user[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Now the core domain question, because it defines the schema and it's the exact thing notifications will key off: what
  makes a task one a user "cares about"?

  - [1mAssignee only[0m — each task has one owner; you hear about your own tasks. Buys the simplest schema and no extra
    tables; costs the common case where a requester wants to track a task they handed off. Widening it later means a
    migration, but a small one.
  - [1mAssignee + creator[0m — both the person doing it and the person who asked hear about it. Buys the two relationships
    almost every tracker needs, still with no join table; costs nothing much. Also migration-cheap to widen.
  - [1mExplicit watchers[0m — a subscription table, so anyone can follow any task, with assignee/creator auto-subscribed. Buys
    arbitrary interest and gives notifications a single clean thing to query; costs a join table, subscribe/unsubscribe
    UI, and lifecycle rules.
  - [1mProject/list membership[0m — you care about everything in a shared list you belong to. Buys team-wide visibility; costs
    a grouping concept and, in practice, per-user mute controls to stop the noise.

  My recommendation is [1mexplicit watchers with assignee and creator auto-subscribed[0m. It's the one model where
  notifications reduce to a single query — "who watches this task" — instead of a growing pile of special cases, and it
  subsumes the first two rather than conflicting with them. The cost is one table and two endpoints, which is small now
  and genuinely awkward to retrofit once notification-fanout logic is written against a narrower rule.
